// The same direct-statement rule a bare `spawn` obeys (tests/xfail/spawn_in_nested_block.kama), for the
// same reason and with a sharper edge: `parallel_spawn` declares a `kama_isolate_t[]` at the SCOPE's own
// depth so the scope-barrier join can name it. One declared inside a nested block is out of scope at the
// join — invalid C, in fully safe kama.
//
// Hoisting would not save it either: the array's extent is a runtime `length()`, so a single hoisted
// declaration cannot serve two `parallel_spawn`s in one scope. A scope's children are what is written
// directly in it, which is also what keeps them countable by reading the block. This MUST fail to compile.
import { std::concurrent::Isolate, std::collections::DynamicArray, std::collections::View };

fn int32 main() {
    DynamicArray<int32> a = DynamicArray.empty();
    a.add(item: 1);
    scope {
        {
            parallel_spawn (ref int32 e in a) { e = e + 1; }   // nested — the join cannot name the array
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
